      "seed": 644587893,
      "version": 532,
      "versionNonce": 337848397,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791466373284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SPLADE-v3",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SPLADE-v3",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "sRe4G9HbA1PiTga2QqhpW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3257.038106167128,
      "y": -57.35950642068468,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "iRW2krLOuH6BW7syw3ulk",
        "o-xsSnyoYkT0IxIbf6kIi",
        "arUtdgh8uXXwNDyQo4p-8",
        "ewM2wijyw-gTBJWJsf_HI",
        "yLJO9IDQhIwFPny6t_BGn",
        "WhxdHxXCiDaRJu5hVeSFr"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 68266523,
      "version": 8016,
      "versionNonce": 999462573,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791466373284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SPLADE-v3 does not reinvent SPLADE; it improves the training recipe. It combines more hard negatives,\nstronger ensemble-based distillation, two complementary distillation losses, and continued fine-tuning\nfrom SPLADE++SelfDistil.\n\nThe key innovations are:\n\nMultiple hard negatives per query\nIn SPLADE v2, we discussed training with a positive passage and a hard negative, with additional in-\nbatch negatives in the contrastive setting. SPLADE-v3 extends training to support multiple explicitly\nsampled hard negatives per query. A larger negative candidate pool exposes the model to more difficult\nand diverse retrieval mistakes. The model no longer learns from only one particular irrelevant document.\n\n\nBetter teacher signals through an ensemble of cross-encoders\nIn SPLADE v2, distillation used a single cross-encoder to generate relevance scores for query–document\npairs.\nSPLADE-v3 instead uses five cross-encoders:\n\n                                                1        MiniLM-L-6\n                                                2        RankT5-3B\n                                                3        DeBERTa-v3\n                                                4        DeBERTa-v2\n                                                5        ELECTRA\n                                                \nThe scores from the different teacher models are normalized per query and combined. \n\nCombining Margin-MSE and KL-divergence\nRecall that SPLADE v2 introduced Margin-MSE, SPLADE-v3 introduces a second distillation objective:\nKL-divergence. KL-divergence encourages the probability distribution over candidate documents produced\nby SPLADE to resemble the teacher's distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhere teacher and student probabilities are obtained by applying softmax to their respective scores.\nThe paper reports an interesting empirical observation:\n- Margin-MSE tended to favor recall, helping retrieve more relevant documents.\n- KL-divergence tended to favor precision, helping prioritize highly relevant documents.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrelease three other SPLADE-v3 variants:\n\n1. SPLADE-v3-DistilBERT8 , which instead starts training from DistilBERT – and thus has a smaller\n    inference “footprint”.\n2. SPLADE-v3-Lexical9 , for which we remove query expansion, thus reducing the retrieval FLOPS (and\n    improving efficiency).\n3. SPLADE-v3-Doc10 , which starts training from CoCondenser, and where no computation is done for\n    the query – which can be seen as a simple binary Bag-of-Words\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SPLADE-v3 does not reinvent SPLADE; it improves the training recipe. It combines more hard negatives, stronger ensemble-based distillation, two complementary distillation losses, and continued fine-tuning from SPLADE++SelfDistil.\n\nThe key innovations are:\n\nMultiple hard negatives per query\nIn SPLADE v2, we discussed training with a positive passage and a hard negative, with additional in-batch negatives in the contrastive setting. SPLADE-v3 extends training to support multiple explicitly sampled hard negatives per query. A larger negative candidate pool exposes the model to more difficult and diverse retrieval mistakes. The model no longer learns from only one particular irrelevant document.\n\n\nBetter teacher signals through an ensemble of cross-encoders\nIn SPLADE v2, distillation used a single cross-encoder to generate relevance scores for query–document pairs.\nSPLADE-v3 instead uses five cross-encoders:\n\n                                                1        MiniLM-L-6\n                                                2        RankT5-3B\n                                                3        DeBERTa-v3\n                                                4        DeBERTa-v2\n                                                5        ELECTRA\n                                                \nThe scores from the different teacher models are normalized per query and combined. \n\nCombining Margin-MSE and KL-divergence\nRecall that SPLADE v2 introduced Margin-MSE, SPLADE-v3 introduces a second distillation objective: KL-divergence. KL-divergence encourages the probability distribution over candidate documents produced by SPLADE to resemble the teacher's distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhere teacher and student probabilities are obtained by applying softmax to their respective scores.\nThe paper reports an interesting empirical observation:\n- Margin-MSE tended to favor recall, helping retrieve more relevant documents.\n- KL-divergence tended to favor precision, helping prioritize highly relevant documents.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nrelease three other SPLADE-v3 variants:\n\n1. SPLADE-v3-DistilBERT8 , which instead starts training from DistilBERT – and thus has a smaller\n    inference “footprint”.\n2. SPLADE-v3-Lexical9 , for which we remove query expansion, thus reducing the retrieval FLOPS (and\n    improving efficiency).\n3. SPLADE-v3-Doc10 , which starts training from CoCondenser, and where no computation is done for\n    the query – which can be seen as a simple binary Bag-of-Words\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
